===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|fédéralisme}}, equivalent to {{af|en|federal|-ism}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A {{l|en|system}} of {{l|en|national}} {{l|en|government}} in which {{l|en|power}} is {{l|en|divided}} between a {{l|en|central}} {{l|en|authority}} and a number of {{l|en|regions}} with [[delimited]] [[self-governing]] authority.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Hudson Meadwell|chapter=8: Quebec Independence and Secession in the Developed West|editor=Sukumar Periwal|title=Notions of Nationalism|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=3bxuEngnpN0C&pg=PA140&dq=%22consociationalism%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=7F3VUf28FuLIiAeLuYBo&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22consociationalism%22&f=false|page=140
|passage=Where consociationalism and '''federalism''' overlay one another, moreover, the continuity of the latter depends on the stability of consociationalism.}}
# The [[belief]] that [[such]] a [[system]] is [[superior]] to [[other]] [[form]]s of [[government]].
# {{lb|en|theology}} {{1|covenantalism}}.

